### What is EC2 and when do you choose it?

EC2 gives you a virtual server. You pick the instance type, the operating system, and you are responsible for everything above the hypervisor: patching the OS, installing runtimes, configuring auto scaling groups, and securing the host. Choose EC2 when you need full control, a specific OS or kernel tuning, licensed software tied to a host, or a long-running process that does not fit a container model cleanly.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.ec2.Ec2Client;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.ec2.model.*;

public class EC2FullControl {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Ec2Client ec2 = Ec2Client.create();
        // Full VM – you control OS, runtime, everything
        RunInstancesRequest req = RunInstancesRequest.builder()
                .imageId("ami-0abcd1234")
                .instanceType(InstanceType.C6I_XLARGE)  // compute optimized for risk engine
                .keyName("nbi-risk-key")
                .minCount(1).maxCount(1).build();
        ec2.runInstances(req);
        // You must: patch OS, install Java, configure autoscaling, monitor
    }
}
```

### What is ECS and when do you choose it?

ECS runs Docker containers. You package the application as an image, define a task definition (CPU, memory, image, environment, IAM task role), and ECS schedules and keeps the desired number of tasks running. It integrates tightly with load balancers, IAM, CloudWatch, and VPC networking. Choose ECS when your team wants containers with minimal operational complexity and is happy to stay within AWS.

```java
// Conceptual ECS task definition in Java
record TaskDefinition(String image, int cpu, int memoryMb, String taskRoleArn) {}
TaskDefinition transfer = new TaskDefinition(
        "123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/transfer-service:1.4.0",
        512, 1024,
        "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/transfer-task-role");
// ECS keeps 3 tasks running, restarts any that fail,
// integrates with ALB for health checks and traffic routing
```

### What is EKS and when do you choose it?

EKS runs Kubernetes, managed by AWS for the control plane while you run workloads on worker nodes or Fargate. You get the full Kubernetes API, Helm charts, operators, and portability across clouds and on-premises. Choose EKS when you need Kubernetes portability, already have Kubernetes expertise and tooling, or must avoid cloud lock-in.

```bash
# Deploy a banking service on EKS
kubectl apply -f - <<EOF
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: settlement-service
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: settlement
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: settlement
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: settlement
        image: 123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/settlement:2.1.0
        resources:
          requests: { cpu: "500m", memory: "1Gi" }
          limits: { cpu: "1", memory: "2Gi" }
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
EOF
```

### What is Lambda and when do you choose it?

Lambda runs functions. You upload code, choose memory (which also scales CPU), and connect triggers such as API Gateway, S3 events, SQS, or EventBridge. AWS runs it only when invoked, scales from zero to thousands automatically, and bills per millisecond. Choose Lambda for short, event-driven, spiky, or infrequent work where you want zero server management and to pay nothing when idle.

```java
import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.Context;
import com.amazonaws.services.lambda.runtime.RequestHandler;
import java.util.Map;

public class StatementUploadHandler implements RequestHandler<Map<String, Object>, String> {
    @Override
    public String handleRequest(Map<String, Object> s3Event, Context context) {
        // Triggered when a statement PDF lands in S3
        // Indexes the statement, notifies the customer
        String bucket = "nbi-customer-statements";
        context.getLogger().log("Processing uploaded statement from " + bucket);
        return "processed";
    }
    // Runs ONLY when invoked – costs nothing when idle
    // Max runtime: 15 minutes
    // Max memory: 10 GB
}
```

### How do they compare side by side?

| Aspect | EC2 | ECS | EKS | Lambda |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Abstraction | Virtual machine | Container task | Kubernetes pod | Function |
| You manage | OS, runtime, scaling, patching | Container image, task definition | K8s manifests, worker nodes | Code, memory setting |
| AWS manages | Hypervisor, hardware | Orchestration, scheduling | Control plane | Everything |
| Scaling | ASG (manual config) | Service auto scaling | HPA / Karpenter | Automatic from zero |
| Billing | Per second (instance runs) | Per task (Fargate) or EC2 | Per pod (Fargate) or EC2 | Per millisecond of execution |
| Max runtime | Unlimited | Unlimited | Unlimited | 15 minutes |
| Startup | Minutes | Seconds | Seconds | Milliseconds to seconds |
| Portability | AWS only | AWS only | Multi-cloud, on-prem | AWS only |
| Best for | Full control, licensed software | Simple AWS-native containers | K8s portability, large ecosystem | Short event-driven work |

